From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 14:29:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] target/ppc: take system calls from problem state without the
 BQL

A PowerPC sc is delivered through cpu_handle_exception(), which holds the
BQL around do_interrupt, and every exception entry and rfi takes it again
in ppc_maybe_interrupt() and, for rfi, in cpu_interrupt_exittb().  A
guest's system calls therefore serialize on the BQL under MTTCG: a
getppid/close storm on an e500mc guest (qemu-system-ppc64) runs at the
same rate at -smp 1 and 4.

Opt in to TCGCPUOps.exception_bql_free for sc (and scv) from problem
state on the BookE and Book3S 64-bit exception models.  Taking one writes
only the vCPU's own state (SRR0/1 or LR/CTR, NIP, MSR, hflags, the
reservation, a pending local TLB flush).  sc 1 on Book3S (the virtual
hypervisor's hypercalls), a nested-HV guest's, a wake-up from power
saving, the other exception models and every other exception keep the
BQL.

ppc_maybe_interrupt() called without the BQL returns early when no
interrupt is pending and CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD is clear: the locked
computation would find nothing and resetting the clear bit is a no-op.
The caller's MSR/LPCR change is ordered before its read of
pending_interrupts and ppc_set_irq() orders its update before it reads
that state (smp_mb() on both sides), so a concurrent ppc_set_irq() either
is seen (and the BQL taken) or sees the new state itself.  This also
covers rfi, mtmsr and wrtee.

cpu_interrupt_exittb() on the vCPU's own thread without icount does what
cpu_interrupt() does there (set the bit atomically, request a TB exit)
without the BQL.

